BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (      Long ago, in the Realm of Echoes, there lived the Weaver and the Keeper. The Weaver held f) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (        ive golden seeds, the only currency of their parched land. If buried in the Keeper's fer) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (        tile soil, the seeds would triple in bounty, but the Weaver feared the Keeper might hoar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (        d the harvest. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      One day, the Weaver approached the Keeper. "I offer all," the Weaver declared, pouring the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (         seeds into the Keeper's calloused palms. "May the harvest bless us both." ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      The Keeper looked at the seeds, then at the Weaver's trembling hands. The Keeper knew that) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (         to keep all meant a fleeting wealth, but to share meant a cycle of abundance that would) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (         endure for generations. The Keeper planted the seeds, nurtured them with water and song) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (        , and watched as the field erupted in gold. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (      When the harvest came, the Keeper returned more than half to the Weaver, honoring the trus) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (        t that had made the growth possible. From that day, the Realm of Echoes transformed into) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (         a garden. They learned that the true value of their currency lay not in the seeds thems) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (        elves, but in the invisible, unbreakable thread of trust woven between them. For in the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (        giving, they found the greatest return.) Tj ET
