BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 718.0 Td (      Here is the myth you wrote in the previous round:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (      Long ago, in the Realm of Echoes, there lived the Weaver and the Keeper. The Weaver held f) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (        ive golden seeds, the only currency of their parched land. If buried in the Keeper's fer) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (        tile soil, the seeds would triple in bounty, but the Weaver feared the Keeper might hoar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (        d the harvest. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (      One day, the Weaver approached the Keeper. "I offer all," the Weaver declared, pouring the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (         seeds into the Keeper's calloused palms. "May the harvest bless us both." ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (      The Keeper looked at the seeds, then at the Weaver's trembling hands. The Keeper knew that) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (         to keep all meant a fleeting wealth, but to share meant a cycle of abundance that would) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (         endure for generations. The Keeper planted the seeds, nurtured them with water and song) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (        , and watched as the field erupted in gold. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (      When the harvest came, the Keeper returned more than half to the Weaver, honoring the trus) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (        t that had made the growth possible. From that day, the Realm of Echoes transformed into) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (         a garden. They learned that the true value of their currency lay not in the seeds thems) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (        elves, but in the invisible, unbreakable thread of trust woven between them. For in the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (        giving, they found the greatest return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (      Here is the myth the other agent wrote in the previous round:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (      In the dawn of the world, the Great Weaver held five strands of celestial silk. She stood ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (        before the Merchant of the Void, who possessed an empty loom. The Weaver knew that if sh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (        e withheld the silk, her hands would remain empty, and the world would stay draped in sh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (        adow. But if she cast the silk into the Merchant's hands, the loom would weave them into) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (         fifteen threads of light.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      "Trust," the Weaver whispered, handing over the five strands. "Use the loom with grace.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      The Merchant, tempted by the glint of potential, could have claimed the fifteen threads fo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (        r his own coffers. Yet, he sensed the heartbeat of the connection between them. He split) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (         the wealth, returning seven threads to the Weaver and keeping eight for the loom's main) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (        tenance. Because he chose honor over avarice, the loom sang, and the next day, the Weave) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (        r arrived with ten strands. The Merchant, emboldened by the fairness of their pact, retu) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (        rned even more.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (      They learned that the multiplier of the universe favors the generous. To hoard is to shrin) Tj ET
